# CLOSED — 0.3.1.0 degrau 25 (cargo/0.3.1.0-degrau-25). This project used to be a KNOWN-STOP pinning
# `needs the null-union wrapper the placement does not declare a type for`, and its own note asked to
# be promoted "back to `When built and run` / `Then exit = 42`, IN THIS SAME PROJECT" the day the gap
# closed. It closed, so it is promoted — the scenario below runs the SAME unchanged `src/probe.tks`
# and asserts its VALUE.
#
# What the stop was. Every own-backend placement coerces its value into the slot's DECLARED type
# (`lower_value_into_type`), except two that had no declared type to coerce against: a struct-literal
# field (the layout table keeps machine classes, not checker types) and a call argument. The note was
# right that "the *placement* carries no type down to lowering" — that was exactly the defect, not a
# property of the language. `collect_field_decls` (the checker-typed companion of the layout table)
# and `callee_param_types` (off `TCall.callee_type`) are what those two placements now read.
#
# The five shapes this note recorded as "CONFIRMED STILL THE SAME STOP" while triaging `bulk`
# (2026-07-29) — a single-level struct-literal `null` field, a self-referential linked-list `next`
# field, an arena-boxed field, a variant-payload field, a recursive-payload field — were all
# struct-literal-field bare `null`s, i.e. all one gap, and all closed by that one fix. The wider
# corpus rows (`union_placement_field_and_argument`, `union_placement_nested_variant`, in
# `own_native`) cover the axes this project does not: the FAT (`str`) union member, a class instance
# field, a bare `T` at a call argument, and a bare variant CASE placed into a `<variant> | null` slot.
#
# NOT superseded by them: this project is the only one that nests a null-union struct INSIDE another
# null-union field, and it is the shape the stop was first measured on.
Feature: native backend — a null-union nested inside a struct field (CLOSED — 0.3.1.0 degrau 25)

  # src/probe.tks — the body authored 2026-07-24 (`1201088`), unchanged since, and green through the
  # C route the whole time. The native backend now lowers it too: both levels present (30), the inner
  # level `null` (fallback 5), the outer level `null` (fallback 7), summing to 42 — so a wrapper
  # written at either level with the wrong tag shows up as a WRONG SUM, not as a stop.
  Scenario: a null-union field nested inside a null-union field now round-trips both levels
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "42"
